WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.776
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:08.370
JASON RANDELL: Hey folks.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:12.750
Welcome to Running a TV
Network in the Cloud, CBS All

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.510
Access on GCP.

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.610
So before we go into
the nitty-gritty,

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:23.370
I just want to address
a little background.

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.690
And so I think one of
the confusing things

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:27.760
is, when you think
about CBS, you generally

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.170
think about the
television network

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:32.479
that we are here to talk about.

00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:38.370
But CBS Interactive is actually
quite a diverse company.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:42.910
We, as the slide demonstrates,
were in the top 10

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:49.810
for traffic, nestled right
between Disney and Comcast.

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:54.310
But our various brands,
we have close to 40

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:56.500
different businesses
under our umbrella.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:02.250
And it drives a tremendous
amount of traffic.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:08.200
But today, what we're here
to talk about is television.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.720
So before we get into
it, what I want to do

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.270
is just talk about,
at a high level,

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:18.268
what we're going to discuss.

00:01:18.268 --> 00:01:20.310
We're going to start off
with where we came from.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:23.800
And this includes the
TV industry itself,

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.780
what it's like for
us as a business

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:31.190
operating in the Cloud, some
of the challenges and lessons

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:35.680
that we faced from
that transition.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.430
And finally, I want
to talk about probably

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:40.820
one of the biggest television
events that happens annually,

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:42.350
and that's the Super Bowl.

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:45.410
So I wanted to talk
about how we handled it,

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:50.150
what the traffic
looked like, and where

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:53.175
we go with those sort
of big tentpole events.

00:01:56.930 --> 00:02:00.740
So where did we come from?

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:06.020
CBS has a rich history
in radio and television,

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:12.650
and we've done an impressive job
moving into the internet space.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:19.430
CBS All Access is the first
direct-to-consumer broadcast

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:21.990
offering.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.680
And we have a lot of
major television events

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:26.790
that we support on our platform.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:29.430
The Super Bowl,
I just mentioned.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:33.890
We also have the Grammys,
March Madness, that really,

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:35.660
if you saw this
last championship,

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:37.800
it was pretty exciting.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.130
And we support our
own original content

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:41.780
as well on the platform.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:45.980
So shows like "Star Trek
Discovery," and the "Twilight

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:49.730
Zone," as well as one
of our bigger offerings

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:52.010
coming back for the
third season, which

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:55.090
is "The Good Fight."

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:58.150
So one of the things
that we have to do

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:02.210
is that there are
expectations around broadcast.

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:05.950
And that is solving the always
on or always ready problem.

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:12.160
The expectation with any
media is that it's there 24/7,

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.230
it's available,
and it's on demand.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:19.910
And what we're faced
with is a background

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:22.550
of really high product delivery.

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:26.390
We've got about 100 years of
innovation in the broadcast

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:27.950
space already.

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:31.820
And that's set the standard
for what the expectations are

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:34.550
for a company that
is doing broadcast,

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:39.650
whether it is through the
traditional broadcast media

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:43.440
or whether it is delivering
that content over the internet.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:50.530
One of the things that we
face is that we're constantly

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:54.310
bombarded by rapid surges
in traffic, meaning that we

00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:56.365
have to scale up very quickly.

00:03:56.365 --> 00:03:58.240
I've mentioned some of
these tentpole events,

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.460
like the Grammys
or the Super Bowl,

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:05.650
and it can be a big challenge
to scale up so quickly.

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:09.400
Live sports broadcast that our
sports team has to deal with

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.710
and that we're often
dealing with ourselves.

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:18.700
As I mentioned, the March
Madness, the championship there

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:24.650
was one of the more exciting
events in just the last week.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:26.650
So we get also call
outs for action.

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:29.710
In previous years, the
Academy of Country Music,

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:32.170
their top award, The
Entertainer of the Year,

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:34.870
was decided on CBS.com.

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:38.770
So that meant a tremendous
surge of activity

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:42.610
during the broadcast, where
one minute, millions of people

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:44.158
would be watching the show.

00:04:44.158 --> 00:04:45.700
And then the next,
millions of people

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:47.860
are coming directly
to our website

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:49.090
in less than five minutes.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:55.930
And then we have the natural
cadence of television.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:05:00.790
So CBS marketing promotes
our premieres, finales,

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:02.320
special events.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:06.040
And this all drive-- can drive
a tremendous amount of traffic

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:07.435
to our platform.

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:12.350
So where did we come from?

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:15.560
And now I talked about the
television broadcasting,

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:19.340
what I want to talk
about is traditionally

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:21.600
our delivery of our platform.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.570
So we were in it--

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:26.630
our own data center.

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:28.580
And we had a service
oriented architecture,

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:34.370
and what we tried to
do is really abstract

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:37.550
each of the layers
of our platform,

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:40.370
that they could
communicate autonomously

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:43.030
and that they were stateless.

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:46.780
This allowed us to do
horizontal scaling very easily

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:50.410
at each level of our
platform, whether that's

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:54.820
our caching layer, our front-end
layer, or our back end.

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:02.600
So one of the things, though,
is that we had a single data

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:03.100
center.

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:08.800
We were not across
multiple data centers

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:11.780
and it was a single zone.

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:13.290
That comes with some advantages.

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:18.690
One of the big advantages
is resource predictability.

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:21.660
So we were running VM
there in the data center,

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:26.220
and we generally knew what
kind of capacity that we had

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:30.610
and what kind of traffic
we were going to expect.

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:33.010
Predictable change control--
in the data center,

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:37.420
we work with the systems
operations group, the network

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:41.370
engineering team, to schedule
any changes inside of the data

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:42.280
center.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:45.790
If the load balancers needed to
be swapped out for a hardware

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:48.550
refresh, we knew when
that was happening,

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:54.150
and we knew we could act
accordingly with our platform.

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:56.130
The engineering
expertise-- as I mentioned,

00:06:56.130 --> 00:07:00.130
inside the network group, inside
the systems operations group,

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:03.480
we were able to really
effectively leverage

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:07.410
that expertise outside of our
application engineering group.

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:13.770
And finally, a simple
cost forecasting.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:17.790
Whether it was hardware
refreshes or models

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:20.310
based on traffic
growth, we knew what

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:23.520
to expect in the data
center every year

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:25.483
through capital expenditures.

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:31.610
So what were the
drivers for the Cloud?

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:34.370
Why did we move
from a model that

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:38.540
was fairly predictable,
that was easy to maintain,

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:41.830
and we had a lot
of expertise in?

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:45.070
So one of them was the
business expectations.

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:51.220
As our application and the
All Access product grew,

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:54.310
there was an expectation
for us to reach

00:07:54.310 --> 00:07:57.310
larger and larger audiences.

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:59.880
And one of the things
is that we didn't

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:03.920
leave any money on the table
when it came to growth.

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:07.220
So we wanted to make sure
that as we were expanding,

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:09.260
we were capturing
the audience, and we

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:11.630
were delivering the product
that audience expected.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:20.160
We wanted to maintain feature
parity with our competitors.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:23.970
For good or bad, Netflix is
the standard in this space.

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:29.460
And we want to show that our
product was a premium product

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:33.035
and it can stand up to any of
the players in the marketplace.

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:38.190
So what were the
technical considerations?

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:41.159
I've already mentioned
that we had a data center.

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:43.390
And that's the single
point of failure.

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:47.310
So we had one pipe going in,
and that same pipe going out.

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:51.180
If that goes down, our entire
platform goes down also.

00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:58.960
Fixed capacity-- we only had so
much space and virtualization

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:00.790
inside of a single--

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:02.590
inside of a single
data center can only

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:05.050
deliver so much capacity.

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:10.340
And at the time when we made the
decision to move to the Cloud,

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:12.050
we were reaching that capacity.

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:17.110
The other part of it is
that while the benefits

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:19.100
of predictability
are good, there

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:22.060
is a real long
procurement process

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:25.180
to get new hardware
swapped in and out.

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:28.975
Moving to the Cloud eliminates
a lot of those problems.

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:36.590
And finally, having
that slowed capacity,

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:39.920
single point of failure,
and that long procurement

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:44.330
process, where we're trying to
get updated hardware in place,

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:46.760
it does slow down the
application development

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:47.870
process.

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:52.010
Ideally, you want to
decouple infrastructure

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:55.555
from application development.

00:09:55.555 --> 00:09:57.410
HANSON WONG: All right.

00:09:57.410 --> 00:09:59.160
All right thank you, Jason.

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:02.690
So operating in the Cloud it--

00:10:02.690 --> 00:10:06.440
for us, it was really
more about organization.

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:11.040
We had a massive cultural
changes within CBSi as a--

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:14.234
both a central
group, as well as a--

00:10:14.234 --> 00:10:18.350
as well as within
our vertical BUs.

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:19.910
So we spent a lot
of time investing

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:25.920
in our teams to up-skill
them, introduce them

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:28.710
to new technologies, send them
to the right training programs.

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:35.160
For us, just taking a
step back, we were really

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:39.160
looking at, just a year
ago, and to some degree,

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:42.330
is still a little bit today, we
have a traditional operations

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:43.270
team.

00:10:43.270 --> 00:10:45.540
You've got one side of
the team that's heavily

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:48.810
focused on systems engineering
and on the other side,

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:51.300
you got the application
team that's constantly

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:54.610
trying to rev and
build out new products,

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:58.160
meet whatever business demand--

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:00.300
whatever the business
demand might be.

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:03.510
So the problem was
it was also separated

00:11:03.510 --> 00:11:06.030
by a wall of compliance.

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:08.530
You have one side that says,
hey, you don't get root access.

00:11:08.530 --> 00:11:11.072
And you get the other side, says
come on give me root access,

00:11:11.072 --> 00:11:12.410
I need to do this really quick.

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:14.915
My product owner wants me to
get this out the door really--

00:11:14.915 --> 00:11:16.830
like, tomorrow.

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:20.970
So we had to kind of take a step
back when we went to the Cloud

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:24.240
and look at where the
engineering boundaries are

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:25.410
going to be.

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:26.870
We like Docker.

00:11:26.870 --> 00:11:28.530
Docker was cool.

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:33.420
It gave engineers the ability
to start making some system

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:36.870
configuration changes, to some
degree, and application server

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:38.040
changes.

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:41.070
And not have to go
through an entire cycle

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:44.870
of putting in a ticket, waiting
for it to be scheduled giving--

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:47.490
getting somebody
to actually perform

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:49.857
that-- perform that task,
and come back and says, hey,

00:11:49.857 --> 00:11:50.940
can you guys check it out?

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:53.220
Does it work?

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:58.200
That entire process
was extremely lengthy.

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:01.540
It slowed down our
ability to pivot.

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:03.910
We just weren't as
nimble as we could.

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:06.210
So going into the
Cloud, we're like OK,

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:09.726
let's take a step back and
let's understand where--

00:12:09.726 --> 00:12:12.720
what are we going to do
to accelerate application

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:15.870
development, but also
maintain the compliance

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:17.800
requirements that we need?

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:20.700
So what we decided
to do was, OK,

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:25.920
within the of the
container, go wild, right?

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:28.940
And those of you that
have worked with--

00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:32.610
worked with Docker knows that
it's not really root, right?

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:34.540
You get access to what
appears to be root,

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:37.130
it's pseudo-root, right?

00:12:37.130 --> 00:12:41.280
So and on the flip side,
on the operation side,

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:45.495
we said, OK, let's start
putting some boundaries in place

00:12:45.495 --> 00:12:47.620
because we don't want
application engineers to say,

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:49.770
hey, I need more
hardware, or more

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:52.620
CPU, more memory, because it
solves my immediate problem,

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:56.040
and it spins up all the costs.

00:12:56.040 --> 00:13:00.180
And we need to put some
boundaries in place

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:04.190
to manage costs and risk.

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:05.770
So with our
traditional ops team,

00:13:05.770 --> 00:13:08.660
we said, OK, so now
we don't want you--

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:12.950
you guys aren't system
engineers anymore.

00:13:12.950 --> 00:13:15.190
You guys aren't
maintaining systems per se.

00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:19.270
You guys have been building
a lot of code to build out

00:13:19.270 --> 00:13:21.520
platforms for
inventory management,

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:24.760
or tools to augment our
ability to do deployments,

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:28.090
or things of that nature.

00:13:28.090 --> 00:13:30.070
You guys are engineers,
you guys have

00:13:30.070 --> 00:13:31.750
been engineers all
this time, you just

00:13:31.750 --> 00:13:33.650
weren't branded as an engineer.

00:13:33.650 --> 00:13:37.630
So we said, OK, why don't we
take a bit of a leap of faith,

00:13:37.630 --> 00:13:40.000
and just start to
transition you guys

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:43.270
into becoming more like SREs.

00:13:43.270 --> 00:13:48.940
SREs being people that are
comfortable and focused

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:50.770
on reliability, right?

00:13:50.770 --> 00:13:54.850
So these guys are
the folks on our team

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:57.910
that was at one point,
system engineers, or SAs,

00:13:57.910 --> 00:13:59.590
transitioning over,
building out code,

00:13:59.590 --> 00:14:04.690
to say, OK, we need to focus
on forward reliability.

00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:06.190
We need to focus
on observability.

00:14:06.190 --> 00:14:08.230
OK, so we're going to
go deploy a platform

00:14:08.230 --> 00:14:10.390
to start collecting metrics.

00:14:10.390 --> 00:14:13.330
We need to start
looking at, how do

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:15.910
we make our infrastructure
more portable,

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:18.280
so that if we have
specific issues

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:20.452
we can move things around?

00:14:20.452 --> 00:14:25.540
So our SRE teams have been--

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:28.540
it's really a transition
from traditional ops

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:34.660
into more of a software space.

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:38.870
So for us, we have both an--

00:14:38.870 --> 00:14:42.620
both vertical SREs, meaning
within our BU, we have SREs,

00:14:42.620 --> 00:14:45.695
and we also have a
centralized SRE team.

00:14:45.695 --> 00:14:49.760
The centralized SRE
team kind of works in--

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:52.460
very closely with our
vertical SRE team.

00:14:52.460 --> 00:14:54.805
They kind of act as
what Google calls--

00:14:54.805 --> 00:14:57.860
and I want to steal
Mike's term here--

00:14:57.860 --> 00:15:01.930
kind of act as CREs
to our SRE team.

00:15:01.930 --> 00:15:04.468
So we have a perpetual
cadence of stuff

00:15:04.468 --> 00:15:07.010
that we need to roll out, that
we've constantly trying to do.

00:15:07.010 --> 00:15:10.940
We leverage a centralized
knowledge base

00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:15.673
of highly skilled individuals
that help us build out

00:15:15.673 --> 00:15:17.090
whatever we need
to build out know

00:15:17.090 --> 00:15:19.260
because they're smart people.

00:15:22.030 --> 00:15:25.950
Why did we choose managed GKE?

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:27.610
Or why did we choose GKE?

00:15:27.610 --> 00:15:31.420
It really comes down to
priorities, like I said before.

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:34.630
We wanted to accelerate
application development.

00:15:34.630 --> 00:15:36.460
And that was kind of
our number one goal.

00:15:36.460 --> 00:15:39.970
And as Jason mentioned,
it was really,

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:44.350
how do we allow individuals
to focus on the application

00:15:44.350 --> 00:15:46.490
and not so much
the infrastructure?

00:15:46.490 --> 00:15:50.890
So we decided to
go with managed GKE

00:15:50.890 --> 00:15:55.490
and focus on ramping up
our teams, number one.

00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:59.830
Number two, redefine a
focus on reliability.

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:03.100
We wanted to make
sure that we started

00:16:03.100 --> 00:16:04.920
adopting some of the process--

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:08.167
some the practices around,
hey, if you build something,

00:16:08.167 --> 00:16:10.000
it functionally works
on your laptop, great.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:11.875
You deploy it, it doesn't
work in production.

00:16:11.875 --> 00:16:12.820
What happened?

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:18.160
So getting that instrumentation
in place, getting that--

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:20.680
getting that
instrumentation in place,

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:23.410
using those reliability
platforms that SRE teams are

00:16:23.410 --> 00:16:25.270
going to be building
out, feeding that back

00:16:25.270 --> 00:16:27.680
into our organization, our
engineering and product

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:30.040
organization to say,
hey, this is a priority.

00:16:30.040 --> 00:16:31.780
You want this to work?

00:16:31.780 --> 00:16:34.000
Fix this, right?

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:36.280
And often, like, when
you're revving feature sets,

00:16:36.280 --> 00:16:39.670
you're not really paying
attention to reliability.

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:42.578
And I think reliability as an
after thought is how you're

00:16:42.578 --> 00:16:43.870
going to get in trouble, right?

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:46.240
You don't want to
think about reliability

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:48.940
in the middle of the
Super Bowl, right?

00:16:48.940 --> 00:16:52.570
Sorry-- cost management GKE.

00:16:52.570 --> 00:16:55.880
We've been able to
leverage the Horizontal Pod

00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:58.970
Autoscaler extensively.

00:16:58.970 --> 00:17:01.750
So we don't get that much
traffic in the middle of night,

00:17:01.750 --> 00:17:03.820
we ramp down our systems.

00:17:03.820 --> 00:17:06.190
Throughout the day,
we have-- we do have

00:17:06.190 --> 00:17:07.637
a lot of peaks and valleys.

00:17:07.637 --> 00:17:09.220
But generally speaking,
we do you also

00:17:09.220 --> 00:17:11.175
have a consistent
oscillating traffic pattern.

00:17:11.175 --> 00:17:13.300
People wake up in the
morning, they watch the news,

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:15.599
they come online,
they go to work,

00:17:15.599 --> 00:17:17.050
they don't watch TV anymore.

00:17:17.050 --> 00:17:19.569
They come to lunch,
they might pop on,

00:17:19.569 --> 00:17:22.290
and tune into their favorite
show, catch 15 minutes of that,

00:17:22.290 --> 00:17:25.310
and so on and so forth.

00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:29.710
And of course, for us, we
have multi-cloud ambitions.

00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:35.740
We definitely believe that you
shouldn't put all your eggs

00:17:35.740 --> 00:17:37.070
in one basket.

00:17:37.070 --> 00:17:39.290
That's why we built
horizontally scalable platform.

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:40.490
That's why we have focus--

00:17:40.490 --> 00:17:42.310
we focus on SOA.

00:17:42.310 --> 00:17:44.980
We want to make sure that--

00:17:44.980 --> 00:17:48.880
you know, if in an event
that we have a high profile

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:53.423
event, tentpole events, we do
have the ability to, say, hey,

00:17:53.423 --> 00:17:55.090
this is not working
for whatever reason.

00:17:55.090 --> 00:17:57.630
Let's go somewhere else.

00:17:57.630 --> 00:18:02.020
Considerations--
it's easy to use.

00:18:02.020 --> 00:18:04.500
I think most of you guys
that have experience

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:06.930
with GKE realized how easy--

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:10.175
how easy it is to pop it
in, run a few commands,

00:18:10.175 --> 00:18:11.550
and next thing
you know, you have

00:18:11.550 --> 00:18:13.980
your service up and running.

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:17.700
Frequent updates--
we want to make sure

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:19.770
that we leverage the
experts as much as we can.

00:18:19.770 --> 00:18:24.660
Google has managed GKE
deployments forever.

00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:27.130
So we want to make
sure that we're

00:18:27.130 --> 00:18:31.950
getting as many updates
to the platform as we can

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:35.380
without us having
to do any work.

00:18:35.380 --> 00:18:37.990
And of course, we have the
thorough support of the Google

00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:41.510
engineering support team and as
well as our technical account

00:18:41.510 --> 00:18:42.010
managers.

00:18:45.010 --> 00:18:47.540
They're constantly pinging
us and saying, hey,

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:48.380
how you guys doing?

00:18:48.380 --> 00:18:49.422
Do you have any problems?

00:18:49.422 --> 00:18:51.942
Do you have any features
that you want us to evaluate?

00:18:51.942 --> 00:18:54.400
Having a managed service allows
us to provide that feedback

00:18:54.400 --> 00:18:57.040
and have leveraged the Google--

00:18:57.040 --> 00:19:00.740
the massive resources that
Google has to do that work.

00:19:03.520 --> 00:19:07.520
Challenges-- so I'm
the problems guy.

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:10.640
So the Cloud wasn't that easy.

00:19:10.640 --> 00:19:13.070
So when we went from a data
center, as Jason mentioned,

00:19:13.070 --> 00:19:15.320
it was a single zone, single
data center, single point

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:20.270
of failure, moving into
a multi-zone environment

00:19:20.270 --> 00:19:23.050
definitely had its challenges.

00:19:23.050 --> 00:19:26.040
So first, when we
were moving over,

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:30.150
we said, hey, we have this
monolithic application, what

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:34.140
can we do to tease this--

00:19:34.140 --> 00:19:37.620
tease this apart,
identify pieces

00:19:37.620 --> 00:19:39.870
that can independently scale?

00:19:39.870 --> 00:19:42.537
We can independently
attach cost to it.

00:19:42.537 --> 00:19:44.370
So we spent a lot of
time finding candidates

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:47.370
for transition.

00:19:47.370 --> 00:19:49.230
SOA obviously, made
it a lot easier.

00:19:49.230 --> 00:19:51.030
Since we're already
service oriented,

00:19:51.030 --> 00:19:53.730
it made life a lot easier
for us to identify the pieces

00:19:53.730 --> 00:19:56.870
that we could move over.

00:19:56.870 --> 00:19:58.670
We didn't want to
lift and shift.

00:19:58.670 --> 00:20:03.110
We obviously went
into containers,

00:20:03.110 --> 00:20:09.980
and we integrated with as many
Cloud-managed products as it

00:20:09.980 --> 00:20:12.110
fit within our architecture.

00:20:12.110 --> 00:20:17.050
There's some things that
didn't, so we ended up

00:20:17.050 --> 00:20:20.170
refactoring our code base,
roughly about 20% to 30%

00:20:20.170 --> 00:20:22.570
of our code bases, to be--

00:20:22.570 --> 00:20:25.210
to accommodate for some
of the integration points.

00:20:25.210 --> 00:20:27.610
We want to make sure have had--

00:20:27.610 --> 00:20:30.350
like we had this one
service that we forked off,

00:20:30.350 --> 00:20:33.615
and it's really a
kind of isolated

00:20:33.615 --> 00:20:35.490
feature of a digital
asset management system.

00:20:35.490 --> 00:20:38.440
It resizes thumbnails.

00:20:38.440 --> 00:20:40.360
Historically, the
source code was built

00:20:40.360 --> 00:20:42.760
to work on a file system, NFS.

00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:45.255
On the Cloud, yeah,
I can have NFS.

00:20:45.255 --> 00:20:48.490
I wouldn't make people
very happy if I could--

00:20:48.490 --> 00:20:52.810
I can have NFS, but it's
more appropriate to integrate

00:20:52.810 --> 00:20:53.590
with object store.

00:20:53.590 --> 00:20:55.720
So we ended up gutting
a lot of that code

00:20:55.720 --> 00:20:58.480
and doing a direct
integration with object store

00:20:58.480 --> 00:21:02.300
so that we can also
have the ability to--

00:21:02.300 --> 00:21:04.270
the regional distribution
of our assets

00:21:04.270 --> 00:21:08.400
and all that fancy stuff.

00:21:08.400 --> 00:21:10.920
Monolithic to microservices,
straddling between the data

00:21:10.920 --> 00:21:14.130
center and GCP.

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:19.950
We didn't do a single hop
jump to for our cutovers.

00:21:19.950 --> 00:21:26.010
We actually end up teasing apart
roughly about 8 microservices

00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:30.510
and slowly started moving
our products up into GCP.

00:21:30.510 --> 00:21:33.730
Obviously, we had concerns
about dockerization.

00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:35.480
We had concerns about,
like, is Kubernetes

00:21:35.480 --> 00:21:37.030
going to work for us?

00:21:37.030 --> 00:21:38.970
What happens when things fail?

00:21:38.970 --> 00:21:39.960
Can we induce failure?

00:21:39.960 --> 00:21:42.480
And how do we respond
to those failures?

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:45.160
We had questions
about all that stuff.

00:21:45.160 --> 00:21:46.050
So what we decided--

00:21:46.050 --> 00:21:49.590
so we started taking teasing
apart these microservices,

00:21:49.590 --> 00:21:53.290
moving them up one at a time,
and just kind of building up--

00:21:53.290 --> 00:21:55.678
building up our own skill set.

00:21:55.678 --> 00:21:56.970
And of course, we had problems.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:00.750
That's a bad look.

00:22:00.750 --> 00:22:02.840
JASON RANDELL: [LAUGHS]

00:22:02.840 --> 00:22:04.590
HANSON WONG: So for
us, it was really more

00:22:04.590 --> 00:22:06.690
of a cutover problem.

00:22:06.690 --> 00:22:13.810
So we'll go back to
the thumbnail resizer.

00:22:13.810 --> 00:22:17.223
We tried to pre-cache
as much as we could.

00:22:17.223 --> 00:22:18.640
So these are the
dynamic resizers.

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:20.330
So when you make a
request, it goes and says,

00:22:20.330 --> 00:22:21.830
hey, I need this
asset at this size,

00:22:21.830 --> 00:22:23.380
and it goes in and makes it.

00:22:23.380 --> 00:22:25.780
And it caches that asset
and makes it available.

00:22:25.780 --> 00:22:28.130
It doesn't resize
multiple times.

00:22:28.130 --> 00:22:30.490
So we tried to pre-cache
as much as we can.

00:22:30.490 --> 00:22:34.530
We loaded all of apps, we ran
through all the permutations.

00:22:34.530 --> 00:22:35.280
Things were great.

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:36.280
The applications work.

00:22:36.280 --> 00:22:38.120
We were able to validate that.

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:41.350
But we'd miscalculated
how many people

00:22:41.350 --> 00:22:44.360
are hot linking our
assets on the internet.

00:22:44.360 --> 00:22:46.790
So what happened was, as
soon as we flipped over--

00:22:46.790 --> 00:22:51.540
that flipped over to DNS, we had
a tremendous amount of traffic

00:22:51.540 --> 00:22:53.260
that just hit us.

00:22:53.260 --> 00:22:57.270
So luckily, because we were
on Kubernetes, we saw--

00:22:57.270 --> 00:23:00.360
we leveraged the
Cloud to allow us to--

00:23:00.360 --> 00:23:02.610
well, if the Cloud has all
the resources in the world.

00:23:02.610 --> 00:23:05.730
So we just scaled
out as big as we

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:08.160
could to absorb that
traffic for a week or two.

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:11.180
And then as the
assets were cached,

00:23:11.180 --> 00:23:12.840
we started bringing
down the cluster.

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:15.240
But without having a
fixed infrastructure,

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:17.490
you wouldn't be able to do
something like that, right?

00:23:17.490 --> 00:23:22.580
You wouldn't have all these
resources at your fingertips.

00:23:22.580 --> 00:23:25.140
So the internal load balancer--

00:23:25.140 --> 00:23:27.920
traffic distribution
is tuple based.

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:32.720
It does not account for
utilization on your back end.

00:23:32.720 --> 00:23:36.041
So this one kind of
bit us a little bit.

00:23:36.041 --> 00:23:40.850
We are accustomed to a stateless
SOA application, right?

00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:43.400
So we expected
this load balancer

00:23:43.400 --> 00:23:45.830
to just absorb our
traffic, round-robin

00:23:45.830 --> 00:23:49.340
our traffic to our back
ends, and awesome, right?

00:23:49.340 --> 00:23:50.870
Well, that didn't quite happen.

00:23:50.870 --> 00:23:55.970
So because they are tuple
based, if you have connections--

00:23:55.970 --> 00:23:59.600
if you have services that make
connections to these services,

00:23:59.600 --> 00:24:02.450
and they're persistent
connections, they would linger.

00:24:02.450 --> 00:24:05.990
And they would stick and be
fixed on to a specific node

00:24:05.990 --> 00:24:08.717
until that connection dies.

00:24:08.717 --> 00:24:10.550
But because we're trying
to optimize and not

00:24:10.550 --> 00:24:12.687
make a bunch of
HTTP requests, we

00:24:12.687 --> 00:24:14.270
decided to use
persistent connections,

00:24:14.270 --> 00:24:16.790
and it just kind of
created a scenario

00:24:16.790 --> 00:24:20.150
where it was leaning
on two of our services.

00:24:20.150 --> 00:24:24.430
In this case, we
have four machines--

00:24:24.430 --> 00:24:26.450
or four pods.

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:29.740
And over time, in the beginning,
when the system is restarted,

00:24:29.740 --> 00:24:32.310
the connection pools
were hydrating.

00:24:32.310 --> 00:24:33.930
They made a bunch
of connections.

00:24:33.930 --> 00:24:36.140
Everything looked great.

00:24:36.140 --> 00:24:37.910
20 minutes later, we get this.

00:24:40.490 --> 00:24:44.330
So obviously, what
happened here was

00:24:44.330 --> 00:24:46.970
that back-end services
with persistent connections

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:50.130
lacked the entropy necessary
for even distribution.

00:24:50.130 --> 00:24:53.540
So we weren't able to get
even distribution with the ILB

00:24:53.540 --> 00:24:54.750
as it stands today.

00:24:54.750 --> 00:24:56.450
We did reach out to Google.

00:24:56.450 --> 00:24:59.410
Google did come back to us and
say, it is a feature request,

00:24:59.410 --> 00:25:01.580
so if you guys have
had this problem,

00:25:01.580 --> 00:25:05.020
please reach out to your TAM.

00:25:05.020 --> 00:25:07.780
We did, but because--

00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:11.680
we can't live with this, we
obviously had to do something.

00:25:11.680 --> 00:25:15.860
So we decided to think about,
what happens if we scale out?

00:25:15.860 --> 00:25:20.030
If we scaled out, we would be
able to create more entropy--

00:25:20.030 --> 00:25:22.610
right-- because the tuples
would have more entropy.

00:25:22.610 --> 00:25:24.680
Therefore, the load would
be more distributed.

00:25:24.680 --> 00:25:27.230
But over time, it
will eventually

00:25:27.230 --> 00:25:30.570
lean against one
or two pods anyway.

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:33.530
So that wasn't
really a solution.

00:25:33.530 --> 00:25:35.820
Application load
balancing-- so we said,

00:25:35.820 --> 00:25:39.080
hey, maybe we can just
do this ourselves.

00:25:39.080 --> 00:25:41.360
Forget using the ILB,
or allow the application

00:25:41.360 --> 00:25:45.200
to make requests directly to
whichever back-end service.

00:25:45.200 --> 00:25:49.790
Well, the issue here is
because we're in the Cloud now,

00:25:49.790 --> 00:25:53.960
resource limitations is not
excuse I can have anymore.

00:25:53.960 --> 00:25:56.080
We need to be able
to support autoscale.

00:25:56.080 --> 00:25:58.320
When you do autoscale,
you have to figure out

00:25:58.320 --> 00:26:02.290
a means for discovery for those
resources that come online

00:26:02.290 --> 00:26:03.250
to become available.

00:26:03.250 --> 00:26:05.800
We have to be able to inject
that into our application.

00:26:05.800 --> 00:26:09.230
Do we really want
to reinvent that?

00:26:09.230 --> 00:26:14.930
So lastly, we said, OK, maybe we
can roll our own load balancer.

00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:20.260
We looked at
products like HAProxy

00:26:20.260 --> 00:26:24.670
and a bunch of
solutions like that.

00:26:24.670 --> 00:26:28.960
And we ended up using the
NGINX Ingress Controller.

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:33.070
And what this allowed us to
do-- now this still uses ILB.

00:26:33.070 --> 00:26:35.620
But what happens is the NGINX--

00:26:35.620 --> 00:26:40.210
so the leaning happens between
the ILB and the NGINX Ingress

00:26:40.210 --> 00:26:41.170
Controller.

00:26:41.170 --> 00:26:42.730
But within NGINX,
it's smart enough

00:26:42.730 --> 00:26:45.070
to understand that,
hey, I need to make

00:26:45.070 --> 00:26:48.740
equal distribution across
the nodes that I'm aware of.

00:26:48.740 --> 00:26:54.010
And so the balancing
ultimately fixes itself, right?

00:26:54.010 --> 00:26:58.600
So more problems, zonal
dependencies and KubeDNS.

00:26:58.600 --> 00:27:03.040
So going into a data
center to multiple zones,

00:27:03.040 --> 00:27:05.830
when you have a
relational database--

00:27:05.830 --> 00:27:09.220
and maybe some of you guys
use MySQL here yourself--

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:11.600
you have a master and you
have bunch of replicas.

00:27:11.600 --> 00:27:13.690
So you have to put the
master somewhere, right?

00:27:13.690 --> 00:27:16.900
So it's either going to be,
in this case, A, B, or C.

00:27:16.900 --> 00:27:19.390
And you have a
situation here now

00:27:19.390 --> 00:27:21.850
that if your application
is running in zone C,

00:27:21.850 --> 00:27:25.970
and you have a dependency on
zone A, if zone A goes away,

00:27:25.970 --> 00:27:27.760
that's problematic, right?

00:27:27.760 --> 00:27:31.330
So we need to kind of fix
some of those questions-- some

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:32.850
of those problems.

00:27:32.850 --> 00:27:34.150
We looked at KubeDNS.

00:27:34.150 --> 00:27:35.980
Like we said, OK,
KubeDNS is cool.

00:27:35.980 --> 00:27:38.852
It understands when
services become available,

00:27:38.852 --> 00:27:40.810
it understands where the
back end services are,

00:27:40.810 --> 00:27:42.530
it goes and distributes that.

00:27:42.530 --> 00:27:48.280
But KubeDNS has-- distributes
across all the zones.

00:27:48.280 --> 00:27:50.550
So when that happens, that's
not really what I want.

00:27:50.550 --> 00:27:53.160
I kind of want a scenario
that kind of looks like this.

00:27:53.160 --> 00:27:55.330
This is a work in progress,
so I don't have a--

00:27:55.330 --> 00:28:00.230
I can't sign off on
a full solution yet.

00:28:00.230 --> 00:28:05.097
But we want to be in a position
where we are zone independent.

00:28:05.097 --> 00:28:06.680
So if we're zone
independent, it means

00:28:06.680 --> 00:28:08.930
that a request that goes
into zone A, stays in zone A,

00:28:08.930 --> 00:28:13.290
gets processed in zone A. And
if that zone goes away, cool.

00:28:13.290 --> 00:28:15.930
We'll just cordon off that
zone, drain everything

00:28:15.930 --> 00:28:18.180
out of-- drain all our
back-end services at that zone,

00:28:18.180 --> 00:28:20.230
and move on.

00:28:20.230 --> 00:28:22.110
So in order for us to
be zone independent,

00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:26.490
we have to move
onto HA databases,

00:28:26.490 --> 00:28:27.730
High-Availability databases.

00:28:27.730 --> 00:28:33.090
So we use Redis
pretty religiously.

00:28:33.090 --> 00:28:34.795
So we started
thinking about, hey,

00:28:34.795 --> 00:28:37.230
are we going to use
the cluster Redis?

00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:41.250
Are we going to use
Redis backed on Sentinel?

00:28:41.250 --> 00:28:43.935
Or do we just deploy a
bunch of different Redises

00:28:43.935 --> 00:28:45.810
and just make the
application aware that they

00:28:45.810 --> 00:28:47.724
need to call specific--

00:28:47.724 --> 00:28:51.630
specific C names
for so that traffic

00:28:51.630 --> 00:28:54.360
can stay within the same zone?

00:28:54.360 --> 00:28:56.370
We looked at
cloud-managed products

00:28:56.370 --> 00:28:58.710
like Spanner and Cloud SQL.

00:28:58.710 --> 00:29:03.020
Unfortunately, they are
regional, so we didn't want--

00:29:03.020 --> 00:29:04.590
or at least Cloud
SQL is regional.

00:29:04.590 --> 00:29:07.350
And we didn't want to be
tied to a specific region.

00:29:07.350 --> 00:29:12.530
We wanted to be able to be
in the US East and US West.

00:29:12.530 --> 00:29:15.650
We are using Bigtable
for a feature set.

00:29:15.650 --> 00:29:18.030
I'll come back to that.

00:29:18.030 --> 00:29:20.045
But we ultimately, wanted
to be cloud neutral.

00:29:20.045 --> 00:29:21.670
We wanted to make
sure that we selected

00:29:21.670 --> 00:29:23.690
a database that allows
us to span multiple

00:29:23.690 --> 00:29:26.040
regions, multiple clouds.

00:29:26.040 --> 00:29:33.110
And so we are
evaluating CockroachDB.

00:29:33.110 --> 00:29:36.800
To maintain traffic
within a single zone,

00:29:36.800 --> 00:29:38.990
we are looking at
service meshes.

00:29:38.990 --> 00:29:41.900
We haven't quite gotten to the
point where we can thoroughly

00:29:41.900 --> 00:29:44.010
talk about this yet.

00:29:44.010 --> 00:29:47.150
We're still evaluating whether
or not Istio or a other

00:29:47.150 --> 00:29:50.660
competitive-- competing
product is the right product--

00:29:50.660 --> 00:29:51.650
product approach.

00:29:51.650 --> 00:29:56.107
But we are looking at
service meshes to do that.

00:29:56.107 --> 00:29:58.190
And obviously, we have a
lot of decisions to make.

00:30:00.710 --> 00:30:04.340
Lessons learned--
plan for failure.

00:30:04.340 --> 00:30:11.300
All of our desire to be zone
independent, be cross-regional,

00:30:11.300 --> 00:30:13.310
multi-cloud, is because
we want to make sure

00:30:13.310 --> 00:30:17.250
that we have the capability
of failing out of a zone.

00:30:17.250 --> 00:30:21.690
We have to make sure that
we have a DR strategy.

00:30:21.690 --> 00:30:24.870
Really focus our teams
on setting up reliability

00:30:24.870 --> 00:30:25.773
as a product.

00:30:25.773 --> 00:30:27.690
You've got to build,
you've got to instrument,

00:30:27.690 --> 00:30:30.170
you've got to evaluate, repeat.

00:30:30.170 --> 00:30:33.770
Everybody's responsibility.

00:30:33.770 --> 00:30:36.770
Focus teams on too much
instrumentation-- nah, no way.

00:30:39.290 --> 00:30:40.580
Create a feedback loop.

00:30:40.580 --> 00:30:44.900
I think this is one of the
bits about building out

00:30:44.900 --> 00:30:48.110
a SRE culture.

00:30:48.110 --> 00:30:49.640
You have to be in
a situation where

00:30:49.640 --> 00:30:54.410
you have a blame-free culture
that allows instrumentation

00:30:54.410 --> 00:30:59.300
to get surfaced, and not have
the person that's reporting

00:30:59.300 --> 00:31:02.270
the issue be blamed
for X, Y, and Z.

00:31:02.270 --> 00:31:06.010
And it has to be well absorbed
by the product engineering

00:31:06.010 --> 00:31:07.280
organizations.

00:31:07.280 --> 00:31:11.070
And it's a team effort.

00:31:11.070 --> 00:31:14.250
That's the bit
about SLO/SLIs here.

00:31:14.250 --> 00:31:17.460
And I'll let Jason talk
about the Super Bowl.

00:31:17.460 --> 00:31:21.610
JASON RANDELL: Yeah, so Hanson
touched on a number of things.

00:31:21.610 --> 00:31:24.960
And I think one of the
most important things to--

00:31:24.960 --> 00:31:29.070
really, to emphasize
is the cultural shift

00:31:29.070 --> 00:31:32.910
when you move from
systems operations groups

00:31:32.910 --> 00:31:35.520
to an SRE mindset.

00:31:35.520 --> 00:31:37.920
And one of the first
things that comes up

00:31:37.920 --> 00:31:41.550
is collaboration with
Google and their SRE team.

00:31:41.550 --> 00:31:46.290
I can't overstate
this enough, that one

00:31:46.290 --> 00:31:50.250
of the benefits of working with
Google Cloud is that Google

00:31:50.250 --> 00:31:54.930
really does want to become
partners with all of the teams

00:31:54.930 --> 00:31:57.140
that come onboard
their platform.

00:31:57.140 --> 00:32:00.510
And that means really
understanding our application,

00:32:00.510 --> 00:32:02.910
really understanding
what our problems are,

00:32:02.910 --> 00:32:05.370
and trying to help
solve those problems.

00:32:05.370 --> 00:32:09.450
One of the things Hanson talked
about with the GKE component

00:32:09.450 --> 00:32:13.710
is that there is a virtuous
feedback cycle with Google,

00:32:13.710 --> 00:32:16.830
where we give them
feedback about our problems

00:32:16.830 --> 00:32:19.320
with their products, they
go back to their product

00:32:19.320 --> 00:32:21.870
and engineering
teams to really focus

00:32:21.870 --> 00:32:25.620
on a lot of these problems,
and improve the services

00:32:25.620 --> 00:32:27.280
for their customers.

00:32:27.280 --> 00:32:29.520
So when we talk
about Super Bowl,

00:32:29.520 --> 00:32:31.860
it's not just our team
getting ready for it,

00:32:31.860 --> 00:32:34.170
it's Google also
getting ready for it.

00:32:34.170 --> 00:32:38.370
So we spent months working
with the Google team getting

00:32:38.370 --> 00:32:41.100
ready for this launch.

00:32:41.100 --> 00:32:47.070
And what that meant is working
with our TAM, our Technical

00:32:47.070 --> 00:32:51.990
Account Manager, working
with the support team

00:32:51.990 --> 00:32:54.480
there at Google.

00:32:54.480 --> 00:32:57.390
And we really gave them
an in-depth understanding

00:32:57.390 --> 00:33:01.770
of our platform, how our service
oriented architecture worked,

00:33:01.770 --> 00:33:04.050
and all of the
different platforms

00:33:04.050 --> 00:33:06.690
that we were serving
this content on.

00:33:06.690 --> 00:33:09.870
I'm going to pause for a
minute and talk about that,

00:33:09.870 --> 00:33:16.170
because I think there's one key
differentiator in our product

00:33:16.170 --> 00:33:19.920
in comparison to a lot of
our broadcast competitors

00:33:19.920 --> 00:33:21.780
that are starting
to come online.

00:33:21.780 --> 00:33:29.310
And that is that we have over
200 affiliate stations that

00:33:29.310 --> 00:33:31.330
have their own local streams.

00:33:31.330 --> 00:33:35.370
So we're delivering the local
CBS station regardless of where

00:33:35.370 --> 00:33:37.740
you are in the United States.

00:33:37.740 --> 00:33:41.160
That means that all of those
stations have to be up.

00:33:41.160 --> 00:33:43.080
We have to have an
operations team,

00:33:43.080 --> 00:33:45.330
and we have to
have a culture that

00:33:45.330 --> 00:33:48.030
make sure that all
of our customers

00:33:48.030 --> 00:33:53.000
are receiving the
product that they want.

00:33:53.000 --> 00:33:55.260
So the Super Bowl
itself-- now I've

00:33:55.260 --> 00:33:57.830
mentioned the 200
local affiliates.

00:33:57.830 --> 00:34:00.670
So it's not just one stream
that we had to worry about.

00:34:00.670 --> 00:34:04.060
It was 200-plus
separate streams.

00:34:04.060 --> 00:34:06.820
And this includes
backup streams as well,

00:34:06.820 --> 00:34:10.449
because one of the most
important considerations

00:34:10.449 --> 00:34:14.739
here is mitigation
and disaster recovery.

00:34:14.739 --> 00:34:18.860
On any event this size, with
your audience expectations,

00:34:18.860 --> 00:34:22.770
you better be prepared
for something to go wrong.

00:34:22.770 --> 00:34:25.370
So what we saw on the
day the Super Bowl

00:34:25.370 --> 00:34:30.239
was greater than 17
times our normal traffic.

00:34:30.239 --> 00:34:32.929
And you can see how
these requests spiked up

00:34:32.929 --> 00:34:35.230
during the day of
the Super Bowl.

00:34:35.230 --> 00:34:39.199
Now we had preplanned,
and as I mentioned,

00:34:39.199 --> 00:34:41.960
one of our big challenges
around these tentpole events

00:34:41.960 --> 00:34:46.639
is that we can have very rapid
request growth in extremely

00:34:46.639 --> 00:34:48.370
short amount of time.

00:34:48.370 --> 00:34:51.650
So always being
prepared for that

00:34:51.650 --> 00:34:56.580
and not relying on autoscale
is probably a safe bet.

00:34:56.580 --> 00:35:01.940
So before the event, we scaled
up 14 times our infrastructure.

00:35:01.940 --> 00:35:04.050
And over the course
of the event,

00:35:04.050 --> 00:35:08.960
I would say about 3x of
the additional scaling

00:35:08.960 --> 00:35:11.120
was then managed
by the autoscaler.

00:35:11.120 --> 00:35:14.300
But that gave us the
ability to really absorb

00:35:14.300 --> 00:35:17.780
that initial request hit
that our systems took

00:35:17.780 --> 00:35:21.450
without missing a
beat on delivery.

00:35:21.450 --> 00:35:23.790
Now the good news here is
that calculating that scale

00:35:23.790 --> 00:35:25.990
was pretty easy.

00:35:25.990 --> 00:35:30.570
What we saw is that one core was
about 50 requests per second.

00:35:30.570 --> 00:35:33.900
And we were able to do the
math and scale accordingly

00:35:33.900 --> 00:35:41.320
so that we were able to deliver
at a really high-quality level.

00:35:41.320 --> 00:35:43.090
Some of the
considerations that we

00:35:43.090 --> 00:35:45.440
had to take in
account besides just

00:35:45.440 --> 00:35:48.940
the massive number of
requests was scaling

00:35:48.940 --> 00:35:51.100
our back-end database systems.

00:35:51.100 --> 00:35:52.870
Hanson showed you a
little bit about some

00:35:52.870 --> 00:35:54.850
of our current challenges.

00:35:54.850 --> 00:35:58.990
Well, what we had to realize is
that as we scaled horizontally,

00:35:58.990 --> 00:36:03.010
each of those cores taking up
50 requests per second, that we

00:36:03.010 --> 00:36:06.430
had to scale those
connections to our database

00:36:06.430 --> 00:36:09.548
in order to handle the
amount of additional load

00:36:09.548 --> 00:36:10.340
that it was going--

00:36:10.340 --> 00:36:13.610
it was going to receive.

00:36:13.610 --> 00:36:17.740
So we had to consider the
upstream services, and just

00:36:17.740 --> 00:36:20.440
the fact that more pods is
going to equal more connections

00:36:20.440 --> 00:36:22.120
to those database systems.

00:36:22.120 --> 00:36:25.120
So especially when you have
a single point of failure,

00:36:25.120 --> 00:36:27.250
like we did with
our master, it's

00:36:27.250 --> 00:36:29.945
a really important consideration
when you're thinking

00:36:29.945 --> 00:36:31.195
about scaling up your systems.

00:36:34.902 --> 00:36:36.610
So I'm going to give
this back to Hanson,

00:36:36.610 --> 00:36:38.940
and he'll finish up with
some of the best practices.

00:36:38.940 --> 00:36:39.898
HANSON WONG: All right.

00:36:39.898 --> 00:36:42.900
So we've got some--
to kind of close off--

00:36:42.900 --> 00:36:44.567
we have some best practices--

00:36:47.460 --> 00:36:49.260
some best practices listed here.

00:36:49.260 --> 00:36:52.980
Number one, test your
infrastructure, please,

00:36:52.980 --> 00:36:54.480
because if you
don't test it, you

00:36:54.480 --> 00:36:59.850
will never know, so especially
your critical services.

00:36:59.850 --> 00:37:01.340
Work with your TAM.

00:37:01.340 --> 00:37:05.710
Your TAMs are there to help you.

00:37:05.710 --> 00:37:07.720
For us, we had a situation
where we say, hey,

00:37:07.720 --> 00:37:11.130
we're going to get
all this traffic.

00:37:11.130 --> 00:37:14.390
Do we have enough resources
allocated to our accounts

00:37:14.390 --> 00:37:17.750
so that we can--

00:37:17.750 --> 00:37:19.960
we can scale to that
point if we needed to?

00:37:19.960 --> 00:37:25.560
So work with them to define what
you believe is the amount of--

00:37:25.560 --> 00:37:28.950
or are the amount of
resources that you need.

00:37:28.950 --> 00:37:30.900
They'll make sure it happens.

00:37:30.900 --> 00:37:34.500
100% engage with
your CREs and SREs.

00:37:34.500 --> 00:37:36.798
Treat them as if they were
part of your own team.

00:37:36.798 --> 00:37:38.465
Do your architectural
reviews, make sure

00:37:38.465 --> 00:37:41.310
that they understand how
each component actually

00:37:41.310 --> 00:37:46.080
works, where your single
points of failure are, if any,

00:37:46.080 --> 00:37:48.350
and where your most
critical components are.

00:37:48.350 --> 00:37:50.820
Share your instrumentation.

00:37:50.820 --> 00:37:52.890
The Google team has a
lot of instrumentation

00:37:52.890 --> 00:37:57.270
behind the scenes that
can really augment

00:37:57.270 --> 00:37:59.550
your perception on how things--

00:37:59.550 --> 00:38:04.620
how things are actually
running from your metrics.

00:38:04.620 --> 00:38:09.180
And runbooks, if you don't
have runbooks, create them.

00:38:09.180 --> 00:38:11.230
You have to plan for failure.

00:38:11.230 --> 00:38:13.800
So if you are going to get
yourself in a situation

00:38:13.800 --> 00:38:18.510
where you may lose a master
database, have a runbook--

00:38:18.510 --> 00:38:21.720
runbook entry in there that
says, hey, if this falls apart,

00:38:21.720 --> 00:38:23.050
what do I do?

00:38:23.050 --> 00:38:23.740
Who do I call?

00:38:23.740 --> 00:38:26.400
Who do I scream at?

00:38:26.400 --> 00:38:28.300
Product development
life-cycles--

00:38:28.300 --> 00:38:31.350
we have to incorporate
reliability.

00:38:31.350 --> 00:38:34.587
And it's going to be
a perpetual cycle.

00:38:34.587 --> 00:38:36.420
You're not going to
able to get around that.

00:38:39.240 --> 00:38:43.200
The whole point of planning
for failure, disaster recovery.

00:38:43.200 --> 00:38:44.970
At the very least, have a plan.

00:38:44.970 --> 00:38:47.760
For us, we have ambitions
of going multi-cloud.

00:38:47.760 --> 00:38:53.790
Hopefully, that will help us
effectively have a disaster--

00:38:53.790 --> 00:38:56.080
DR strategy.

00:38:56.080 --> 00:38:58.440
But yeah, I think that's it.

00:38:58.440 --> 00:39:01.790
[MUSIC PLAYING]

